1. What is terraform.tfstate?
terraform.tfstate is a state file that Terraform uses to keep track of the infrastructure it manages.
It stores:Resource IDs
          Resource attributes
          Metadata
          Resource dependencies
          Current infrastructure state

Example:
If Terraform creates an EC2 instance:
resource "aws_instance" "web" {
  instance_type = "t2.micro"
}

Terraform stores information like:
aws_instance.web
instance_id = i-123456789
public_ip = 13.x.x.x
inside the state file.
Without the state file, Terraform would not know which resources it already created.

2. Why is terraform.tfstate needed?
Terraform uses the state file to compare:

Desired State (Terraform Code)
            VS
Current State (State File)

When we run:
terraform plan

Terraform checks:
What exists in the state file
What exists in the code

Then it calculates what needs to be created, modified, or deleted.
Without the state file:Terraform loses resource tracking.
                      Infrastructure drift becomes difficult to manage.
                      Updates become unreliable.
3. What happens if two engineers run terraform apply simultaneously?
Imagine:Engineer A:
terraform apply
Engineer B:terraform apply
Both are using the same infrastructure.

Problems can occur:State corruption
                  Resource conflicts
                  Duplicate resources
                  Inconsistent state

Example:
Engineer A updates EC2
Engineer B updates Security Group
Both try to update the state file at the same time.

Result:
State file corruption
       or
Last writer wins
which can overwrite changes.

4. Why use S3 + DynamoDB?
Architecture:
Terraform
    │
    ▼
S3 Bucket
(State Storage)
    │
    ▼
DynamoDB
(State Locking)
S3

Stores the shared Terraform state file.
Benefits:Centralized state
          Versioning
          Backup
          Team access

Example:
terraform.tfstat
is stored in:
s3://terraform-state-bucket/
instead of a developer's laptop.

DynamoDB
Provides state locking.
Before Terraform modifies state:

Acquire Lock
      ↓
Update State
      ↓
Release Lock
Only one person can modify state at a time.

5. Why is Remote State Used?
Remote state allows multiple engineers to share a single Terraform state file.
Without remote state:

Engineer A Laptop
     state
Engineer B Laptop
     state

Each engineer has a different copy.
This causes:State mismatch
            Resource drift
            Team collaboration issues

With remote state:
Team
  │
  ▼
S3 Bucket
  │
Shared State
Everyone uses the same state file.
Benefits:Team collaboration
         Centralized management
         Version control
         Disaster recovery
         CI/CD integration
6. Why is State Locking Needed?
Imagine: Current EC2: t2.micro
Engineer A:
changes to t3.micro
Engineer B:
changes to t3.small

Both run: terraform apply at the same time.
Terraform could write conflicting updates.
State locking prevents this.
Process:
Acquire Lock
     ↓
Run Apply
     ↓
Update State
     ↓
Release Lock
Other users must wait until the lock is released.

7. What Problem Does DynamoDB Solve?
DynamoDB provides a locking mechanism that prevents multiple Terraform operations from modifying the state file simultaneously.
Without DynamoDB:

Multiple terraform apply
       ↓
State corruption

With DynamoDB:
Engineer A acquires lock
Engineer B waits
This ensures:Consistency
             Safe collaboration
             No state corruption

8. Explain Root Module vs Child Module
Terraform projects are built using modules.
Root Module: The directory where Terraform commands are executed.
Example:

project/
│
├── main.tf
├── variables.tf
├── outputs.tf

Commands:terraform init
terraform apply are run here.
This is called the Root Module.

Child Module
Reusable Terraform code called by the root module.
Example:
modules/
└── ec2/
    ├── main.tf
    ├── variables.tf
    └── outputs.tf

Root Module calls Child Module:
module "ec2" {
  source = "./modules/ec2"
  instance_type = "t2.micro"
}

Architecture:
Root Module
    │
    ├── Child Module (EC2)
    │
    ├── Child Module (VPC)
    │
    └── Child Module (RDS)
Why Use Child Modules?
Benefits:Reusability
         Less code duplication
         Easier maintenance
         Standardization
Example:
Instead of writing EC2 code 20 times:
Dev
QA
Prod
you write one EC2 module and reuse it.

Short Interview Version (30-Second Answers)
What is terraform.tfstate?
Stores the current state of infrastructure managed by Terraform and maps resources in code to actual cloud resources.

Why Remote State?
To allow multiple team members and CI/CD pipelines to share a single source of truth for infrastructure state.

Why S3?
To centrally store Terraform state with durability, versioning, and team access.

Why DynamoDB?
To provide state locking and prevent concurrent Terraform operations from corrupting the state file.

Root Module vs Child Module?
The root module is where Terraform commands are executed. Child modules are reusable Terraform components called by the root module.
